{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "לא תאכלו על הדם", :translit "lo tocheilu al ha-dam", :gloss "you shall not eat on the blood"} {:he "לערלה", :translit "le-orlah", :gloss "to orlah"} {:he "שאסור לאכול בלא ברכה", :translit "she-asur le-echol b'li berachah", :gloss "that it is forbidden to eat without a blessing"} {:he "כערלה בלא חילול", :translit "k'orlah b'li chilul", :gloss "like orlah without clearance"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Smikhut parshiyot: the verse א לא תאכלו על הדם stands adjacent to laws of ערלה in the preceding section, so the latter casts light on the former."}], :frame {:text "The practice of deriving a prohibition against eating without a berakhah from scriptural adjacency is rooted in Berakhot 35a, where Rav Yehudah cites Rav that it is forbidden to derive benefit from this world without a berakhah, and the Talmud derives this from the verse לה' הארץ ומלואה (Psalms 24:1) and its counterpart in the Temple-era verse (Leviticus 19:23–24) where produce is forbidden until its fourth year. The parallel to orlah as something forbidden until an act of חילול is drawn from Leviticus 19:23–24.", :sources ["Berakhot 35a" "Leviticus 19:23-24" "Psalms 24:1"]}} {:quotes [{:he "לא תאכלו על הדם", :translit "lo tocheilu al ha-dam", :gloss "you shall not eat on the blood"} {:he "לא תנחשו", :translit "lo t'nacheshu", :gloss "you shall not practice divination"} {:he "שלא ינחשו באכילה", :translit "she-lo y'nachshu b'achilah", :gloss "that they not divine through eating"} {:he "כדרך שעושין הרוצחים שאוכלים לחם על ההרוג", :translit "k'derekh she-osin ha-rotz'chim she-och'lim lechem al ha-harug", :gloss "in the manner that murderers do, who eat bread over the slain"} {:he "שלא ינקמו גואלי ההרוג", :translit "she-lo yink'mu go'elei ha-harug", :gloss "so that the blood-avengers of the slain not take vengeance"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Smikhut parshiyot: לא תאכלו על הדם is immediately followed by לא תנחשו, linking the two prohibitions."}], :frame {:text "The phrase לחם על ההרוג (bread over the slain) as a ritual of the first murderer is attested in Sanhedrin 37b, where Rab and R. Hanina disagree on the verse's referent: one identifies it as the generation of the flood, the other as the generation of the dispersion. The imagery of eating bread over a murdered body and the use of גואל הדם for the blood-avenger draws on the legal framework of Numbers 35:19.", :sources ["Sanhedrin 37b" "Numbers 35:19"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Smikhut parshiyot: the verse א לא תאכלו על הדם stands adjacent to laws of ערלה in the preceding section, so the latter casts light on the former.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Smikhut parshiyot: לא תאכלו על הדם is immediately followed by לא תנחשו, linking the two prohibitions.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :leviticus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:24:31.957265489Z", :model "glm-5.2"}